    "question": {
      "dom_id": "question-107046",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent and $-{v}_{3}=2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2},$ which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. ${v}_{1}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0$ has only the zero solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$\n\nIII. $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly dependent set",
      "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, solving the equation $-{v}_{3}=2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}$ for ${v}_{1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}{v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{3}.\n$$\n\nThis means that ${v}_{1}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $-{v}_{3}=2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2},$ we have that\n\n$$\n2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}+1\\cdot {v}_{3}=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0$ has a non-zero solution.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0$ has a non-zero solution, the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  and III only.\"",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Incorrect",
